    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nPrinciple of mixtures: Heat lost by hot water equals heat gained by cold water: m₁ c (T₁ - T) = m₂ c (T - T₂).\n\n2. GIVEN:\nm₁ = 100 g, T₁ = 80°C, m₂ = 100 g, T₂ = 20°C, c = 1 cal/(g·°C).\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nT = (m₁ T₁ + m₂ T₂) / (m₁ + m₂)\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Heat lost by hot water: Q_lost = 100 × 1 × (80 - T).\n2. Heat gained by cold water: Q_gain = 100 × 1 × (T - 20).\n3. Equating: 100(80 - T) = 100(T - 20).\n4. (100 + 100) T = 8000 + 2000 = 10000.\n5. T = 10000 / 200 = 50.00°C.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: 50.00°C\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nWhen masses are not equal, the equilibrium temperature is a weighted average biased towards the larger mass!",
