    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nStep 1: Check if heat available from water cooling to 0°C exceeds heat required to melt all ice. Step 2: If yes, remaining heat warms the total mass (m_water + m_ice) above 0°C.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nm_ice = 10 g at 0°C, m_water = 100 g at 40°C, L_f = 80 cal/g.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nQ_gain = m_ice L_f + m_ice c_w T, Q_lost = m_water c_w (T_w - T)\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Heat to melt all ice: Q_melt = 10 × 80 = 800 cal.\n2. Heat released by water cooling to 0°C: Q_avail = 100 × 1 × 40 = 4000 cal.\n3. Since 4000 > 800, all ice melts completely!\n4. Remaining heat: ΔQ = 4000 - 800 = 3200 cal.\n5. Total mass of water = 110 g.\n6. Final temperature T = 3200 / 110 = 29.09°C.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: 29.09°C\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nAlways compare Q_available to Q_melt before calculating final temperature!",
